---
description: Engineering rules for the Pickens My Jeanty CBS draft agent
alwaysApply: true
---

# Non-negotiable rules

- Correctness > autonomy > aesthetics.
- Never guess CBS selectors. Use Playwright MCP/codegen against the real page.
- Keep CBS reader and CBS executor separate.
- Never store or request CBS credentials in source/config/env.
- The user logs in manually in a visible persistent browser.
- Default execution mode is `recommend`, not `autopilot`.
- Every browser action must have a precondition and postcondition.
- Every successful pick must be verified from CBS draft results.
- Do not retry a pick click after verification ambiguity.
- The OpenAI model can select only among deterministic shortlist candidate IDs.
- Any out-of-shortlist model output is invalid and triggers deterministic fallback.
- Do not send raw CBS HTML/chat/ads to OpenAI; send normalized structured state.
- No OCR if DOM/accessibility data exists.
- Do not invent SportsLine fields. The supplied workbook contains rating, player, ADP, round, bye week.
- Write tests for pure draft logic before browser integration.
- Use Zod at all external boundaries.
- Keep event logs append-only and free of secrets.
- Keep live browser automation fail-closed.
- Never bypass platform authentication/access controls.

# Code style

- TypeScript strict mode.
- Prefer small pure functions in `engine/`.
- Use discriminated unions for state-machine events/errors.
- No `any` except tightly isolated third-party parsing boundaries, followed immediately by validation.
- Avoid magic weights: load them from strategy config.
- Log component scores so recommendations are explainable.

# Delivery sequence

Implement phases in `docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` in order. Do not jump to full autopilot before read-only and confirm-mode validation.
